course: dictionary
lesson: 7412
title: Poke: push, peek and tease
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Poke mostly means to push something with your finger or a pointed object. «A boy in Texas woke his hamster in the daytime by poking it; the startled hamster bit his finger hard.» The hamster jumped and bit because it was pushed while it was sleeping, so poking is a sharp, quick push.
explain: Poke has other meanings too. A poke is a quick push. To poke out means to stick out so part of something shows, and to poke around means to look in a place, sometimes where you should not. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Context can also tell you whether poking is safe. «You must never poke a knife or fork into a toaster, even when it is switched off at the wall.» The word never is a clue that this is a safety rule, because poking into electrical things can hurt you badly.
fact: To poke means to push with a finger or a pointed object.
fact: A poke is a quick push.
fact: «Poke fun at» means to laugh at someone in an unkind way.
fact: «Poke your nose into» means to be nosy, looking into things that are not yours.
fact: Never poke things into sockets, your ears, your eyes or an animal, because it can hurt them or you.
exercise: «Never poke things into sockets, and never touch plugs with wet hands.» What is the safe rule here? | Touch plugs with wet hands to be quick | Poke the socket every day to keep it clean | Poke things into sockets to see if they work | *Keep your fingers and objects out of sockets
exercise: Lily says, «He poked fun at my new coat.» What does poked fun at mean? | To give him a surprise present | *To laugh at him unkindly | To push him gently | To look at him very closely
exercise: «The fox's head poked out of the hole.» What does poked out mean? | Fell asleep inside | Ran away very fast | *Stuck out so you could see part of it | Dug a deep tunnel
test: «The chimpanzees poke sticks into a fake termite mound to fish out honey.» What do the chimps do with the sticks? | Break them into small pieces | *Push them in to get honey out | Throw them at the other chimps | Use them to build a new home
test: Dad says, «Don't poke your nose into my desk.» What does he mean? | Tidy up the room for me | Give me a big hug | Move my desk to the other room | *Stop being nosy and looking through my things
exam: «Ben will poke fun at anyone who sings in class.» What does poke fun at mean here? | Look around for them | Hide from them | Push them with a pencil | *Laugh at them unkindly
